toml_config / css / latex. Net -24: 21 type-arg-adjacent errors drained,
two REAL defects revealed and fixed, one pre-existing narrowing corrected.

TWO REAL CRASH PATHS. tree_sitter.Node.text is `bytes | None`, which is
invisible while the `node` parameter is untyped. Annotating it showed
toml_config.py HALF-GUARDING ITS OWN IDIOM:

  93:  return node.text.decode(...)                        # unguarded
  104: return node.text.decode(...) if node.text else ""   # guarded
  111: text  = node.text.decode(...) if node.text else ""  # guarded

Same file, same pattern, and only the unguarded half can raise
AttributeError on a text-less key node. Both now follow the file's own
majority convention. This IS a behaviour change (crash -> empty string);
it was chosen because two sibling lines already made exactly that call,
so matching them is a smaller claim than inventing a policy.

THE GUARD RULE MATTERS MORE THAN THE ANNOTATIONS. _get_string_value
carries an `if node is None:  # pragma: no cover - defensive` guard, so
it takes `Node | None` while its five siblings take plain `Node`.
Annotating it non-Optional would turn a live guard into an [unreachable]
error -- growing a different code and breaking the shrink-only gate, or
forcing deletion of a real guard. A `no cover` pragma means UNTESTED, not
UNREACHABLE. The span-ternary revert earlier in this cycle is what
getting that distinction wrong costs.

tree_sitter enters css.py and toml_config.py as a TYPE_CHECKING-only
import. Not stylistic: tree-sitter is an optional dependency for these
analyzers, and a module-scope import converts a graceful "grammar
unavailable, skip" into an ImportError at startup.

Also: scala.py narrows `_sym is not None` and then discarded the narrowed
local, re-reading `lookup_result.symbol.id`. Same object at runtime; now
uses `_sym.id`.

Baseline ratcheted 821 -> 797. 6932 tests pass.

